Allow readv/writev in the LibSandbox seccomp policy.

The file-descriptor seccomp policy introduced in ba87a32626 ("LibSandbox:
Add reusable sandbox building blocks") whitelists read/write/pread64/pwrite64
but not the vectored readv/writev syscalls. Once ea1ecdad5a ("RequestServer:
Sandbox the process on Linux") started applying this policy to RequestServer,
any TLS-heavy connection crashes the process with SIGSYS: curl/OpenSSL read
socket data with readv(), which the policy traps ("Sandbox violation:
disallowed syscall (19)"). This manifests as RequestServer becoming
unavailable and the WebContent RequestClient constructor tripping a VERIFY,
so most HTTPS sites fail to load.

readv/writev are universally available on Linux, so the plain
SECCOMP_APPEND_ALLOW_SYSCALL macro (which does not require an IF_DEFINED_*
helper) is used.

This is unfixed upstream as of the packaged revision, and Ladybird no longer
accepts external patches, so it is carried downstream. Remove if upstream
adds readv/writev to the policy.
--- a/Libraries/LibSandbox/Seccomp.cpp
+++ b/Libraries/LibSandbox/Seccomp.cpp
@@ -882,6 +882,8 @@
 {
     SECCOMP_APPEND_ALLOW_SYSCALL_IF_DEFINED(*this, read);
     SECCOMP_APPEND_ALLOW_SYSCALL_IF_DEFINED(*this, write);
+    SECCOMP_APPEND_ALLOW_SYSCALL(*this, readv);
+    SECCOMP_APPEND_ALLOW_SYSCALL(*this, writev);
     SECCOMP_APPEND_ALLOW_SYSCALL_IF_DEFINED(*this, close);
     SECCOMP_APPEND_ALLOW_SYSCALL_IF_DEFINED(*this, fstat);
     SECCOMP_APPEND_ALLOW_SYSCALL_IF_DEFINED(*this, dup);
